Design Robinhood's stock lending program. Users can opt individual holdings into a lending pool; the system matches those shares against borrower demand (e.g. short sellers) and pays the lending user interest for as long as their shares are on loan. Critically, the user must still be able to see their shares as fully owned and sell them at any time, so the system needs to recall loaned shares (or substitute collateral) transparently without blocking or delaying the user's own trade.
